IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `disc-dates`

-- Discrimination Act 1991 (ACT) -- the questions of the person who has been discriminated against, sexually harassed,
-- vilified or victimised, and who is thinking of complaining.
--
-- SEAT: the individual on the receiving end: the job applicant or employee, the contract worker or commission agent,
-- the student, the customer, the tenant or other occupier, the club member, the sportsperson, the person dealing with
-- an ACT public authority; and the person who is harassed, vilified or victimised. "Is what was done to me unlawful?",
-- "Is my attribute protected?", "Can they rely on an exception?", "Must they adjust for me?", "Are they answerable for
-- what their employee did?". The person complained about, the Human Rights Commission, the ACAT and the Minister get no
-- seat: what the other side may rely on is asked as a fact or a judgment, and s 70 puts the onus of an exception on
-- them.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, a number, a date, a choice from a list). No question takes a DEONTIC or an
-- EVENT. The one duty owed to the person that the Act states as a "must" with steps to be taken -- the insurer's or
-- super fund's duty to give access to the data it relied on (s 28 (3)) -- is a regulative rule run by EVALTRACE inside
-- the question; it has no deadline in the Act.
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: Part 1 ss 4A, 5AA (as the gloss), 5B (as the gloss of unjustifiable hardship); Part 2 whole (ss 7,
-- 8); Part 3 whole (ss 10-23C); Part 4 whole as a screen of which exceptions could reach the area and ground, and ss 24,
-- 25, 26, 27, 28, 32, 33B, 33C, 41, 46, 51 (2), 52, 53, 54, 57, 57B, 57M worked out from facts; Part 5 whole (ss 58-67);
-- Part 7 whole (ss 67A, 68, 69); Part 8 whole (ss 70-73); Part 9 whole (ss 74-76); s 109 (as what an exemption covers);
-- s 121A; the dictionary's "irrelevant criminal record". Out, and why: NOTES.md.
--
-- Edition: Discrimination Act 1991, A1991-81, Republication No 54 (R54), effective 23 February 2026, deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/A1991-81.txt. The Discrimination Regulation 2016 is not deposited: what the Act leaves
-- to it (further disabilities, prescribed sporting activities) is asked as a fact.
--
-- (c) Australian Capital Territory. Used under CC BY 4.0 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md). Unofficial text.
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. Judgments the Act leaves to the Human Rights Commission or the ACAT
-- (reasonable, proportionate and justifiable; unjustifiable hardship; reasonably feels offended) are INPUTS. Answers
-- follow the rulings on negatives of 2026-11-04 and 2026-11-05 (NOTES.md): an answer that is the text's own no is marked
-- "(the text's no)"; an answer where the text is limited by its own wording, or silent, is marked "NOTHING-kind".

§ `Discrimination Act 1991: the questions of the person discriminated against, harassed, vilified or victimised`

§§ `Part 2 -- Protected attributes and the meaning of discrimination (ss 7, 8)`

-- s 7 (1): the 24 protected attributes, in the Act's order.
DECLARE `Protected attribute` IS ONE OF
    `accommodation status`
    `age`
    `association with a person identified by another protected attribute`
    `breastfeeding`
    `disability`
    `employment status`
    `gender identity`
    `genetic information`
    `immigration status`
    `industrial activity`
    `irrelevant criminal record`
    `parent, family, carer or kinship responsibilities`
    `physical features`
    `political conviction`
    `pregnancy`
    `profession, trade, occupation or calling`
    `race`
    `record of a sex alteration under the Births, Deaths and Marriages Registration Act 1997`
    `relationship status`
    `religious conviction`
    `sex`
    `sex characteristics`
    `sexuality`
    `subjection to domestic or family violence`
    `some other attribute`

-- s 7 (2): how the attribute attaches to the person.
DECLARE `How the attribute is mine` IS ONE OF
    `I have it`
    `I had it in the past`
    `I am thought to have it`
    `I am thought to have had it in the past`
    `it is a characteristic people with the attribute generally have`
    `it is a characteristic people with the attribute are generally presumed to have`
    `none of these links`

DECLARE `Is it a protected attribute` IS ONE OF
    `yes: a protected attribute under s 7`
    `no: not one of the attributes s 7 (1) lists (the text's no)`
    `not shown: s 7 (2) says what the attribute includes without closing the list (NOTHING-kind)`

-- s 7 (1) names "a protected attribute" by a closed list (fork F1: an attribute outside it is the text's no). s 7 (2)
-- "includes" the attribute had, thought to be had, had in the past, and the characteristics people with it generally
-- have or are presumed to have; a link outside s 7 (2) is NOTHING-kind (the list is not closed).
@export Is the ground I was treated on a protected attribute under the Discrimination Act 1991 (ACT) s 7? The Act protects 24 attributes (age, race, sex, disability, pregnancy, religious conviction, sexuality, gender identity, an irrelevant criminal record, employment status and others), whether you have the attribute, had it, or are thought to have or have had it, and the characteristics people with it generally have or are presumed to have.
GIVEN `the attribute` IS A `Protected attribute` @desc The attribute you say you were treated unfavourably on the ground of, from the list in s 7 (1); "some other attribute" if none fits
      `how it is mine` IS A `How the attribute is mine` @desc How the attribute relates to you: you have it, had it in the past, are thought to have it or to have had it, or it is a characteristic people with it generally have or are presumed to have
GIVETH A `Is it a protected attribute`
`s 7 -- is the ground a protected attribute` `the attribute` `how it is mine` MEANS
    IF `the attribute` EQUALS `some other attribute`
    THEN `no: not one of the attributes s 7 (1) lists (the text's no)`
    ELSE IF `how it is mine` EQUALS `none of these links`
         THEN `not shown: s 7 (2) says what the attribute includes without closing the list (NOTHING-kind)`
         ELSE `yes: a protected attribute under s 7`

-- Dictionary, "irrelevant criminal record": "a record relating to an offence, or an alleged offence, if" (a)-(g).
DECLARE `Record of the offence` IS ONE OF
    `charged, and the proceeding is not finalised`
    `charged, and the charge lapsed, was withdrawn or discharged, or was struck out`
    `acquitted`
    `convicted, and the conviction was quashed or set aside`
    `served with an infringement notice for it`
    `convicted, and the conviction is not spent or extinguished`
    `convicted, and the conviction is extinguished under the Spent Convictions Act 2000`
    `convicted, and the conviction is spent under the Spent Convictions Act 2000`
    `none of these records`

-- (e) a conviction whose circumstances are not directly relevant to the situation; (g) a spent conviction unless both
-- its circumstances are directly relevant and the Spent Convictions Act 2000 s 16 does not apply to the person in the
-- situation. A spent conviction is also a conviction under (e). The definition is a "means ... if": outside it, the
-- text's no.
@export Is my criminal record an "irrelevant criminal record", so that discrimination because of it can be unlawful (Discrimination Act 1991 (ACT) dictionary, s 7 (1) (k))? It is if you were charged and the case is not finished or was dropped, you were acquitted, a conviction was quashed, you got an infringement notice, the conviction is extinguished, the conviction's circumstances are not directly relevant to the situation, or the conviction is spent (unless its circumstances are directly relevant and Spent Convictions Act s 16 does not apply to you there).
GIVEN `the record` IS A `Record of the offence` @desc What the record is about: a charge not yet finalised, a charge dropped, an acquittal, a quashed conviction, an infringement notice, a conviction (not spent, extinguished or spent)
      `directly relevant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the circumstances of the offence are directly relevant to the situation in which you were treated unfavourably (for example a conviction for fraud and a job handling money)
      `spent convictions s 16 does not apply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a spent conviction only: whether the Spent Convictions Act 2000 s 16 (which lets you not disclose a spent conviction) does NOT apply to you in this situation (Spent Convictions Act s 19 lists those situations, such as some jobs with children); otherwise FALSE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `dictionary -- is my record an irrelevant criminal record` `the record` `directly relevant` `spent convictions s 16 does not apply` IF
    CONSIDER `the record`
    WHEN `charged, and the proceeding is not finalised` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `charged, and the charge lapsed, was withdrawn or discharged, or was struck out` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `acquitted` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `convicted, and the conviction was quashed or set aside` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `served with an infringement notice for it` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `convicted, and the conviction is not spent or extinguished` THEN NOT `directly relevant`
    WHEN `convicted, and the conviction is extinguished under the Spent Convictions Act 2000` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `convicted, and the conviction is spent under the Spent Convictions Act 2000` THEN
        NOT (`directly relevant` AND `spent convictions s 16 does not apply`)
    WHEN `none of these records` THEN FALSE

DECLARE `Kind of discrimination` IS ONE OF
    `direct and indirect discrimination (s 8 (2), (3))`
    `direct discrimination (s 8 (2))`
    `indirect discrimination (s 8 (3))`
    `no discrimination: the condition is reasonable in the circumstances (s 8 (4)) (the text's no)`
    `no discrimination under s 8 (the text's no)`

-- s 8 (2): direct discrimination is treating, or proposing to treat, a person unfavourably because the person has 1 or
-- more protected attributes; s 4A (2): the attribute need only be one of the reasons, not the dominant one; s 4A (1):
-- doing an act includes failing to do it. s 8 (3): indirect discrimination is imposing, or proposing to impose, a
-- condition or requirement that has, or is likely to have, the effect of disadvantaging the person because of the
-- attribute; s 8 (4): not if the condition is reasonable in the circumstances (a judgment, asked; s 8 (5) lists what is
-- weighed). "a person directly discriminates ... if" is a definition: outside it, the text's no.
GIVEN `unfavourable` IS A BOOLEAN, `because of it` IS A BOOLEAN, `condition imposed` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `condition disadvantages me` IS A BOOLEAN, `condition reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Kind of discrimination`
`the kind of discrimination` `unfavourable` `because of it` `condition imposed` `condition disadvantages me` `condition reasonable` MEANS
    IF `direct` AND `indirect`
    THEN `direct and indirect discrimination (s 8 (2), (3))`
    ELSE IF `direct`
         THEN `direct discrimination (s 8 (2))`
         ELSE IF `indirect`
              THEN `indirect discrimination (s 8 (3))`
              ELSE IF `condition imposed` AND `condition disadvantages me`
                   THEN `no discrimination: the condition is reasonable in the circumstances (s 8 (4)) (the text's no)`
                   ELSE `no discrimination under s 8 (the text's no)`
    WHERE
        `direct` MEANS `unfavourable` AND `because of it`
        `indirect` MEANS `condition imposed` AND `condition disadvantages me` AND (NOT `condition reasonable`)

@export Was I discriminated against, directly or indirectly (Discrimination Act 1991 (ACT) s 8, s 4A)? Direct discrimination is being treated (or about to be treated) unfavourably because you have a protected attribute, even if it was only one of the reasons. Indirect discrimination is having a condition or requirement imposed that disadvantages you because of a protected attribute, unless the condition is reasonable in the circumstances.
GIVEN `unfavourable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the other person treated you, or proposed to treat you, unfavourably (including by failing or refusing to do something)
      `because of it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they did so because you have one or more protected attributes (it is enough that the attribute was one of the reasons, even if not the main one)
      `condition imposed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they imposed, or proposed to impose, a condition or requirement on you (a rule, policy or practice, for example a full-time-only rule or a height requirement)
      `condition disadvantages me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that condition has, or is likely to have, the effect of disadvantaging you because you have a protected attribute
      `condition reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the condition is reasonable in the circumstances, a judgment for the Commission or the ACAT, weighing the nature and extent of the disadvantage, whether it could be overcome or reduced, and whether it is disproportionate to the result sought (s 8 (5))
GIVETH A `Kind of discrimination`
`s 8 -- was I discriminated against` `unfavourable` `because of it` `condition imposed` `condition disadvantages me` `condition reasonable` MEANS
    `the kind of discrimination` `unfavourable` `because of it` `condition imposed` `condition disadvantages me` `condition reasonable`

§§ `Part 3 -- Where discrimination is unlawful (ss 10-23C)`

-- Who discriminated, in which area of life. One constructor per section of Part 3 (s 10 split by its subsections).
DECLARE `Area of life` IS ONE OF
    `s 10 (1): an employer, when I sought employment`
    `s 10 (2): my employer, while I am or was employed`
    `s 12: a principal, and I am or sought to be their commission agent`
    `s 13: a principal, and I am a contract worker doing work for them`
    `s 14: partners, or people forming a partnership`
    `s 15: an organisation of employers or employees`
    `s 16: a body that confers a qualification or authorisation needed for a profession, trade or occupation`
    `s 17: an employment agency`
    `s 18: an educational authority`
    `s 19: a person controlling premises the public may enter or use`
    `s 20: a provider of goods, services or facilities`
    `s 21: a person providing accommodation (as principal or agent)`
    `s 22: a club or its committee of management`
    `s 23: a person asking me for information for a purpose that would be unlawful`
    `s 23A: a person organising or administering a formally organised sporting activity`
    `s 23B: a person organising or administering a formally organised competition`
    `s 23C: a public authority administering a territory law or an ACT government program or policy`
    `none of these areas`

-- The work areas of Division 3.1 and the other areas of Division 3.2.
GIVEN `the area` IS AN `Area of life`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the area is in Division 3.1` `the area` IF
    elem `the area` (LIST `s 10 (1): an employer, when I sought employment`, `s 10 (2): my employer, while I am or was employed`, `s 12: a principal, and I am or sought to be their commission agent`, `s 13: a principal, and I am a contract worker doing work for them`, `s 14: partners, or people forming a partnership`, `s 15: an organisation of employers or employees`, `s 16: a body that confers a qualification or authorisation needed for a profession, trade or occupation`, `s 17: an employment agency`)

GIVEN `the area` IS AN `Area of life`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the area is in Division 3.2` `the area` IF
        (NOT (`the area is in Division 3.1` `the area`))
    AND (NOT (`the area` EQUALS `none of these areas`))

GIVEN `the area` IS AN `Area of life`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the area is in Part 3` `the area` IF
    NOT (`the area` EQUALS `none of these areas`)

DECLARE `Does Part 3 cover it` IS ONE OF
    `covered: Part 3 makes discrimination in this area, by this act, unlawful (unless an exception or exemption applies)`
    `not covered: s 23C (2) takes this act of a public authority outside s 23C (the text's no)`
    `not covered: Part 3 does not reach this area or this act (NOTHING-kind)`

-- Each section of Part 3 lists the acts it makes unlawful (refusing, the terms offered, dismissing, expelling,
-- evicting, "subjecting ... to any other detriment" and so on); whether the act is one the section lists is asked.
-- An act outside a section's list, or an area outside Part 3, is a prohibition limited in its own wording:
-- NOTHING-kind. s 23C (2) (b) "does not ... apply to" (proceedings of the Legislative Assembly, an ACT court's
-- non-administrative acts, an education act under s 18) is a flat negative: the text's no.
GIVEN `the area` IS AN `Area of life`, `a listed act` IS A BOOLEAN, `s 23C (2) excludes it` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Does Part 3 cover it`
`the Part 3 coverage` `the area` `a listed act` `s 23C (2) excludes it` MEANS
    IF `the area` EQUALS `s 23C: a public authority administering a territory law or an ACT government program or policy` AND `s 23C (2) excludes it`
    THEN `not covered: s 23C (2) takes this act of a public authority outside s 23C (the text's no)`
    ELSE IF `the area is in Part 3` `the area` AND `a listed act`
         THEN `covered: Part 3 makes discrimination in this area, by this act, unlawful (unless an exception or exemption applies)`
         ELSE `not covered: Part 3 does not reach this area or this act (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Does Part 3 of the Discrimination Act 1991 (ACT) cover where I was discriminated against (ss 10-23C)? It covers work (employers, principals of commission agents and contract workers, partnerships, unions and employer bodies, qualifying bodies, employment agencies), education, public premises, goods, services and facilities, accommodation, clubs, requests for information, formally organised sport and competitions, and ACT public authorities administering a law, program or policy, each for the acts the section lists.
GIVEN `the area` IS AN `Area of life` @desc Who discriminated against you, and in what capacity (your employer, a school, a shop, a landlord, a club, a public authority and so on); "none of these areas" for a private or social setting
      `a listed act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether what they did is one of the acts that section lists: for example refusing you, the terms or conditions offered, denying or limiting a benefit, dismissing, expelling or evicting you, or subjecting you to any other detriment (most sections end with "any other detriment")
      `s 23C (2) excludes it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a public authority only: whether the act was the Legislative Assembly's office acting in a proceeding of the Assembly, a court or tribunal acting other than administratively, or an education matter covered by s 18; otherwise FALSE
GIVETH A `Does Part 3 cover it`
`Part 3 -- does the Act cover where I was discriminated against` `the area` `a listed act` `s 23C (2) excludes it` MEANS
    `the Part 3 coverage` `the area` `a listed act` `s 23C (2) excludes it`

DECLARE `Religious practice answer` IS ONE OF
    `unlawful: refusing permission for this religious practice in working hours is discrimination under s 11`
    `not shown under s 11: the practice does not meet the conditions s 11 sets (s 10 (2) may still apply) (NOTHING-kind)`

-- s 11: refusing an employee permission to carry out a religious practice during working hours, "being a practice"
-- (a) recognised as necessary or desirable by people of the same religious conviction, (b) reasonable to perform in
-- working hours in the circumstances, (c) not subjecting the employer to unreasonable detriment. The conditions limit
-- the prohibition in its own wording: outside them, NOTHING-kind (fork F2).
@export Is my employer's refusal to let me carry out a religious practice during working hours unlawful (Discrimination Act 1991 (ACT) s 11)? It is if people of your religious conviction recognise the practice as necessary or desirable, performing it in working hours is reasonable in the circumstances of the job, and it does not subject the employer to unreasonable detriment.
GIVEN `refused permission` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your employer refused you permission to carry out a religious practice during working hours
      `recognised as necessary or desirable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether people of the same religious conviction as yours recognise the practice as necessary or desirable (for example daily prayer)
      `reasonable in working hours` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether performing the practice during working hours is reasonable, having regard to the circumstances of the employment (a judgment for the Commission or the ACAT)
      `no unreasonable detriment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the practice does NOT subject the employer to unreasonable detriment (a judgment for the Commission or the ACAT)
GIVETH A `Religious practice answer`
`s 11 -- may my employer refuse my religious practice at work` `refused permission` `recognised as necessary or desirable` `reasonable in working hours` `no unreasonable detriment` MEANS
    IF `refused permission` AND `recognised as necessary or desirable` AND `reasonable in working hours` AND `no unreasonable detriment`
    THEN `unlawful: refusing permission for this religious practice in working hours is discrimination under s 11`
    ELSE `not shown under s 11: the practice does not meet the conditions s 11 sets (s 10 (2) may still apply) (NOTHING-kind)`

§§ `Part 4 -- Exceptions the other side may rely on (ss 24-57R; s 70 puts the onus on them)`

-- Every exception in Part 4, in the Act's order. The screen below says which could reach the area and the ground.
DECLARE `Part 4 exception` IS ONE OF
    `s 24 domestic duties in the employer's home`
    `s 25 residential care of children`
    `s 25A adoption decisions`
    `s 26 domestic accommodation, and accommodation by charitable or voluntary bodies`
    `s 26A preselection by employment agencies`
    `s 27 measures intended to achieve equality`
    `s 28 insurance and superannuation`
    `s 30 acts done necessarily to comply with a territory law or an order`
    `s 31 clubs and voluntary bodies for people sharing an attribute`
    `s 32 religious bodies`
    `s 33A charging for accommodation, goods or services`
    `s 33B genuine occupational qualifications`
    `s 33C inherent requirements of employment`
    `s 33D competitions limited to an age group`
    `s 35 employment of a couple`
    `s 36 single-sex educational institutions`
    `s 37 rights or privileges for pregnancy, childbirth or breastfeeding`
    `s 38 services that can only be provided to one sex`
    `s 39 accommodation for employees, contract workers or students`
    `s 41 single-sex competitive sport`
    `s 44 religious workers`
    `s 45 political workers`
    `s 46 religious educational institutions`
    `s 50 qualifying bodies and disability`
    `s 51 educational institutions and disability`
    `s 52 inaccessible premises`
    `s 53 goods and services provided in a special way`
    `s 54 accommodation needing special services or facilities`
    `s 56 public health`
    `s 57 competitive sport and disability`
    `s 57B youth wages`
    `s 57C health and safety at work (age)`
    `s 57E minimum-age admissions to education`
    `s 57G legal incapacity because of age`
    `s 57H benefits and concessions because of age`
    `s 57J health and safety for goods, services and facilities (age)`
    `s 57K recreational tours and accommodation (age)`
    `s 57M competitive sport limited to an age group`
    `s 57N profession, trade, occupation or calling`
    `s 57O employment status`
    `s 57P immigration status`
    `s 57R physical features and health, safety or property`

`every Part 4 exception` MEANS LIST
    `s 24 domestic duties in the employer's home`, `s 25 residential care of children`, `s 25A adoption decisions`,
    `s 26 domestic accommodation, and accommodation by charitable or voluntary bodies`, `s 26A preselection by employment agencies`,
    `s 27 measures intended to achieve equality`, `s 28 insurance and superannuation`,
    `s 30 acts done necessarily to comply with a territory law or an order`, `s 31 clubs and voluntary bodies for people sharing an attribute`,
    `s 32 religious bodies`, `s 33A charging for accommodation, goods or services`, `s 33B genuine occupational qualifications`,
    `s 33C inherent requirements of employment`, `s 33D competitions limited to an age group`, `s 35 employment of a couple`,
    `s 36 single-sex educational institutions`, `s 37 rights or privileges for pregnancy, childbirth or breastfeeding`,
    `s 38 services that can only be provided to one sex`, `s 39 accommodation for employees, contract workers or students`,
    `s 41 single-sex competitive sport`, `s 44 religious workers`, `s 45 political workers`, `s 46 religious educational institutions`,
    `s 50 qualifying bodies and disability`, `s 51 educational institutions and disability`, `s 52 inaccessible premises`,
    `s 53 goods and services provided in a special way`, `s 54 accommodation needing special services or facilities`,
    `s 56 public health`, `s 57 competitive sport and disability`, `s 57B youth wages`, `s 57C health and safety at work (age)`,
    `s 57E minimum-age admissions to education`, `s 57G legal incapacity because of age`, `s 57H benefits and concessions because of age`,
    `s 57J health and safety for goods, services and facilities (age)`, `s 57K recreational tours and accommodation (age)`,
    `s 57M competitive sport limited to an age group`, `s 57N profession, trade, occupation or calling`, `s 57O employment status`,
    `s 57P immigration status`, `s 57R physical features and health, safety or property`

-- Short lists of areas used by the screen.
`seeking work` MEANS LIST `s 10 (1): an employer, when I sought employment`, `s 13: a principal, and I am a contract worker doing work for them`
`employment` MEANS LIST `s 10 (1): an employer, when I sought employment`, `s 10 (2): my employer, while I am or was employed`
`work positions` MEANS LIST `s 10 (1): an employer, when I sought employment`, `s 10 (2): my employer, while I am or was employed`, `s 12: a principal, and I am or sought to be their commission agent`, `s 13: a principal, and I am a contract worker doing work for them`, `s 14: partners, or people forming a partnership`
`hiring` MEANS LIST `s 10 (1): an employer, when I sought employment`, `s 12: a principal, and I am or sought to be their commission agent`, `s 13: a principal, and I am a contract worker doing work for them`, `s 14: partners, or people forming a partnership`

-- Which area each exception can reach (by the sections of Part 3 it names), and on which ground. Where an exception
-- names only some paragraphs of a section (s 24: s 10 (1) (a), (b) and s 13 (b)), the screen takes the whole section:
-- the screen says what COULD apply, and each exception's own question decides. Fork F3.
GIVEN `the exception` IS A `Part 4 exception`, `the area` IS AN `Area of life`, `the ground` IS A `Protected attribute`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the exception could reach` `the exception` `the area` `the ground` IF
    CONSIDER `the exception`
    WHEN `s 24 domestic duties in the employer's home` THEN elem `the area` `seeking work`
    WHEN `s 25 residential care of children` THEN elem `the area` `seeking work`
    WHEN `s 25A adoption decisions` THEN `the area` EQUALS `s 23C: a public authority administering a territory law or an ACT government program or policy`
    WHEN `s 26 domestic accommodation, and accommodation by charitable or voluntary bodies` THEN `the area` EQUALS `s 21: a person providing accommodation (as principal or agent)`
    WHEN `s 26A preselection by employment agencies` THEN `the area` EQUALS `s 17: an employment agency`
    WHEN `s 27 measures intended to achieve equality` THEN `the area is in Part 3` `the area`
    WHEN `s 28 insurance and superannuation` THEN `the area` EQUALS `s 20: a provider of goods, services or facilities`
    WHEN `s 30 acts done necessarily to comply with a territory law or an order` THEN `the area is in Part 3` `the area`
    WHEN `s 31 clubs and voluntary bodies for people sharing an attribute` THEN elem `the area` (LIST `s 22: a club or its committee of management`, `s 20: a provider of goods, services or facilities`)
    WHEN `s 32 religious bodies` THEN `the area is in Part 3` `the area`
    WHEN `s 33A charging for accommodation, goods or services` THEN elem `the area` (LIST `s 20: a provider of goods, services or facilities`, `s 21: a person providing accommodation (as principal or agent)`)
    WHEN `s 33B genuine occupational qualifications` THEN elem `the area` `work positions` AND (NOT (`the ground` EQUALS `religious conviction`))
    WHEN `s 33C inherent requirements of employment` THEN `the area` EQUALS `s 10 (1): an employer, when I sought employment`
    WHEN `s 33D competitions limited to an age group` THEN `the area` EQUALS `s 23B: a person organising or administering a formally organised competition` AND `the ground` EQUALS `age`
    WHEN `s 35 employment of a couple` THEN elem `the area` `employment` AND `the ground` EQUALS `relationship status`
    WHEN `s 36 single-sex educational institutions` THEN `the area` EQUALS `s 18: an educational authority` AND `the ground` EQUALS `sex`
    WHEN `s 37 rights or privileges for pregnancy, childbirth or breastfeeding` THEN `the area is in Part 3` `the area` AND `the ground` EQUALS `sex`
    WHEN `s 38 services that can only be provided to one sex` THEN `the area` EQUALS `s 20: a provider of goods, services or facilities` AND `the ground` EQUALS `sex`
    WHEN `s 39 accommodation for employees, contract workers or students` THEN
            (    elem `the area` (LIST `s 10 (2): my employer, while I am or was employed`, `s 13: a principal, and I am a contract worker doing work for them`)
             AND elem `the ground` (LIST `sex`, `relationship status`, `pregnancy`, `breastfeeding`))
         OR (    elem `the area` (LIST `s 18: an educational authority`, `s 21: a person providing accommodation (as principal or agent)`)
             AND `the ground` EQUALS `sex`)
    WHEN `s 41 single-sex competitive sport` THEN `the area` EQUALS `s 23A: a person organising or administering a formally organised sporting activity` AND `the ground` EQUALS `sex`
    WHEN `s 44 religious workers` THEN elem `the area` `hiring` AND `the ground` EQUALS `religious conviction`
    WHEN `s 45 political workers` THEN elem `the area` `hiring` AND `the ground` EQUALS `political conviction`
    WHEN `s 46 religious educational institutions` THEN
        elem `the area` (LIST `s 18: an educational authority`, `s 10 (1): an employer, when I sought employment`, `s 10 (2): my employer, while I am or was employed`, `s 13: a principal, and I am a contract worker doing work for them`)
        AND `the ground` EQUALS `religious conviction`
    WHEN `s 50 qualifying bodies and disability` THEN `the area` EQUALS `s 16: a body that confers a qualification or authorisation needed for a profession, trade or occupation` AND `the ground` EQUALS `disability`
    WHEN `s 51 educational institutions and disability` THEN `the area` EQUALS `s 18: an educational authority` AND `the ground` EQUALS `disability`
    WHEN `s 52 inaccessible premises` THEN `the area` EQUALS `s 19: a person controlling premises the public may enter or use` AND `the ground` EQUALS `disability`
    WHEN `s 53 goods and services provided in a special way` THEN `the area` EQUALS `s 20: a provider of goods, services or facilities` AND `the ground` EQUALS `disability`
    WHEN `s 54 accommodation needing special services or facilities` THEN `the area` EQUALS `s 21: a person providing accommodation (as principal or agent)` AND `the ground` EQUALS `disability`
    WHEN `s 56 public health` THEN `the area is in Part 3` `the area` AND `the ground` EQUALS `disability`
    WHEN `s 57 competitive sport and disability` THEN `the area` EQUALS `s 23A: a person organising or administering a formally organised sporting activity` AND `the ground` EQUALS `disability`
    WHEN `s 57B youth wages` THEN elem `the area` `employment` AND `the ground` EQUALS `age`
    WHEN `s 57C health and safety at work (age)` THEN elem `the area` `work positions` AND `the ground` EQUALS `age`
    WHEN `s 57E minimum-age admissions to education` THEN `the area` EQUALS `s 18: an educational authority` AND `the ground` EQUALS `age`
    WHEN `s 57G legal incapacity because of age` THEN `the area is in Part 3` `the area` AND `the ground` EQUALS `age`
    WHEN `s 57H benefits and concessions because of age` THEN `the area is in Division 3.2` `the area` AND `the ground` EQUALS `age`
    WHEN `s 57J health and safety for goods, services and facilities (age)` THEN elem `the area` (LIST `s 19: a person controlling premises the public may enter or use`, `s 20: a provider of goods, services or facilities`) AND `the ground` EQUALS `age`
    WHEN `s 57K recreational tours and accommodation (age)` THEN elem `the area` (LIST `s 20: a provider of goods, services or facilities`, `s 21: a person providing accommodation (as principal or agent)`) AND `the ground` EQUALS `age`
    WHEN `s 57M competitive sport limited to an age group` THEN `the area` EQUALS `s 23A: a person organising or administering a formally organised sporting activity` AND `the ground` EQUALS `age`
    WHEN `s 57N profession, trade, occupation or calling` THEN `the area is in Part 3` `the area` AND `the ground` EQUALS `profession, trade, occupation or calling`
    WHEN `s 57O employment status` THEN elem `the area` `employment` AND `the ground` EQUALS `employment status`
    WHEN `s 57P immigration status` THEN `the area is in Part 3` `the area` AND `the ground` EQUALS `immigration status`
    WHEN `s 57R physical features and health, safety or property` THEN `the area is in Part 3` `the area` AND `the ground` EQUALS `physical features`

@export Which exceptions in Part 4 of the Discrimination Act 1991 (ACT) could the other side rely on, given where I was discriminated against and on what ground (ss 24-57R)? Lists every exception that names that area of Part 3 and that ground (or any ground). The other side must establish an exception (s 70); whether one actually applies turns on its own conditions.
GIVEN `the area` IS AN `Area of life` @desc Who discriminated against you, and in what capacity (your employer, a school, a shop, a landlord, a club, a sporting body, a public authority and so on)
      `the ground` IS A `Protected attribute` @desc The protected attribute you were treated unfavourably on the ground of
GIVETH A LIST OF `Part 4 exception`
`Part 4 -- which exceptions could the other side rely on` `the area` `the ground` MEANS
    filter (GIVEN `an exception` YIELD `the exception could reach` `an exception` `the area` `the ground`) `every Part 4 exception`

-- s 24 and s 25: s 10 (1) (a) or (b) (who is offered employment) or s 13 (b) (not allowing a contract worker to work)
-- does not make discrimination unlawful for a position whose duties involve domestic duties where the first person
-- lives, if reasonable, proportionate and justifiable (s 24); or the care of a child where the child lives (s 25).
@export Can the person who would employ me in their home rely on the domestic-duties or child-care exception (Discrimination Act 1991 (ACT) ss 24, 25)? Only for deciding who gets the job (or whether a contract worker may work), where the duties are domestic duties on the premises where that person lives and the discrimination is reasonable, proportionate and justifiable, or where the duties involve caring for a child where the child lives.
GIVEN `about who gets the job` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the discrimination was in the arrangements for deciding, or in deciding, who should be offered the job, or in not allowing you as a contract worker to work (not your pay or conditions once employed)
      `domestic duties in their home` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the duties of the position involve domestic duties on the premises where the person who would employ you lives
      `reasonable, proportionate and justifiable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the discrimination is reasonable, proportionate and justifiable in the circumstances (a judgment for the Commission or the ACAT)
      `caring for a child in the child's home` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the duties of the position involve caring for a child where the child lives
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `ss 24, 25 -- can they rely on the domestic or child-care exception` `about who gets the job` `domestic duties in their home` `reasonable, proportionate and justifiable` `caring for a child in the child's home` IF
        `about who gets the job`
    AND (    (`domestic duties in their home` AND `reasonable, proportionate and justifiable`)
         OR  `caring for a child in the child's home`)

-- s 26 (1) (a): accommodation where the provider (or a near relative or carer) lives and intends to keep living on the
-- premises, for not more than 6 people besides them; (b) accommodation by a charitable or voluntary body for members
-- of a relevant class of people; s 26 (2): accommodation status, if reasonable having regard to any relevant factors.
@export Can the person providing my accommodation rely on the domestic-accommodation exception (Discrimination Act 1991 (ACT) s 26)? Yes if they (or a near relative or carer) live on the premises and intend to keep living there and the accommodation is for no more than 6 people besides them; or a charitable or voluntary body provides it for a class of people sharing a protected attribute; or the ground is your accommodation status and the discrimination is reasonable.
GIVEN `they live there and mean to stay` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person providing the accommodation, or a near relative (parent, child, grandparent, grandchild, brother, sister or a domestic partner of any of them) or a carer of that person, lives on the premises and intends to continue living there
      `people accommodated besides them` IS A NUMBER @desc How many people the accommodation in the premises is for, not counting the provider and their near relatives or carers who live there
      `charitable body for a class` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a charitable or voluntary body provides the accommodation for members of a class of people identified by a protected attribute (for example a women's refuge)
      `ground is accommodation status` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were treated unfavourably because of your accommodation status (being a tenant or occupant, receiving or waiting for housing assistance, or being homeless)
      `reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For accommodation status: whether the discrimination is reasonable, having regard to any relevant factors such as its effect on you (a judgment for the Commission or the ACAT)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 26 -- can they rely on the domestic accommodation exception` `they live there and mean to stay` `people accommodated besides them` `charitable body for a class` `ground is accommodation status` `reasonable` IF
        (`they live there and mean to stay` AND `people accommodated besides them` AT MOST 6)
    OR  `charitable body for a class`
    OR  (`ground is accommodation status` AND `reasonable`)

-- s 27 (1): Part 3 does not make an act unlawful if a purpose of the act is equal opportunities for, or meeting the
-- special needs of, members of a relevant class; s 27 (2): not if the act discriminates against a member of that class
-- in a way that is not reasonable for achieving that purpose.
@export Can the other side say what they did was a special measure to achieve equality (Discrimination Act 1991 (ACT) s 27)? Yes if a purpose of the act is to give a class of people who share a protected attribute equal opportunities, or access to facilities, services or opportunities that meet their special needs, unless the act discriminates against a member of that class in a way that is not reasonable for that purpose.
GIVEN `a purpose is equality or special needs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a purpose of the act was to ensure that members of a class of people identified by a protected attribute have equal opportunities, or to give them access to facilities, services or opportunities meeting their special needs (for example a women-only training course)
      `I am in that class` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are yourself a member of that class of people
      `unreasonable against me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you are a member of the class: whether the act discriminates against you in a way that is not reasonable for achieving that purpose (a judgment for the Commission or the ACAT)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 27 -- can they say it was a special measure` `a purpose is equality or special needs` `I am in that class` `unreasonable against me` IF
        `a purpose is equality or special needs`
    AND (NOT (`I am in that class` AND `unreasonable against me`))

-- s 28 (2): Part 3 does not make discrimination by an insurer or super fund in the terms of an annuity, insurance policy
-- or fund membership unlawful if (a) based on actuarial or statistical data, or, when that is unavailable, other
-- relevant documents; (b) reasonable to rely on them; (c) reasonable, proportionate and justifiable.
DECLARE `What the terms were based on` IS ONE OF
    `actuarial or statistical data`
    `other relevant documents, actuarial and statistical data being unavailable`
    `other documents, although actuarial or statistical data was available`
    `nothing of that kind`

@export Can my insurer or super fund rely on the insurance and superannuation exception for the terms it offered me (Discrimination Act 1991 (ACT) s 28 (2))? Only if the discrimination is based on actuarial or statistical data (or, where none is available, other relevant documents), it is reasonable for the insurer or fund to rely on them, and the discrimination is reasonable, proportionate and justifiable.
GIVEN `the basis` IS A `What the terms were based on` @desc What the insurer or fund based the different terms on (for example a higher premium or an exclusion because of age or disability)
      `reasonable to rely on it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is reasonable for the insurer or fund to rely on that data or those documents (a judgment for the Commission or the ACAT)
      `reasonable, proportionate and justifiable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the discrimination is reasonable, proportionate and justifiable in the circumstances (a judgment for the Commission or the ACAT)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 28 (2) -- can my insurer or fund rely on the exception` `the basis` `reasonable to rely on it` `reasonable, proportionate and justifiable` IF
        elem `the basis` (LIST `actuarial or statistical data`, `other relevant documents, actuarial and statistical data being unavailable`)
    AND `reasonable to rely on it`
    AND `reasonable, proportionate and justifiable`

-- s 28 (3): "If the consumer requests access to the data or other documents, the insurance or superannuation services
-- provider must -- (a) give the consumer a copy of the data or other documents, or a meaningful explanation of the data
-- or other documents in writing; or (b) make the data or other documents available for inspection at a reasonable time
-- and place." A regulative rule with three ways of doing it (ROR) and no deadline (the Act states none: finding 5).
DECLARE `Party` IS ONE OF
    `the insurer or super fund`

DECLARE `Step` IS ONE OF
    `give me a copy of the data or documents`
    `give me a meaningful written explanation of the data or documents`
    `make the data or documents available for inspection at a reasonable time and place`
    `something else`

GIVETH A DEONTIC `Party` `Step`
`s 28 (3) -- give access to the data relied on` MEANS
        (PARTY `the insurer or super fund` MUST `give me a copy of the data or documents` HENCE FULFILLED)
    ROR (PARTY `the insurer or super fund` MUST `give me a meaningful written explanation of the data or documents` HENCE FULFILLED)
    ROR (PARTY `the insurer or super fund` MUST `make the data or documents available for inspection at a reasonable time and place` HENCE FULFILLED)

DECLARE `What the provider has done` IS ONE OF
    `gave me a copy of the data or documents`
    `gave me a meaningful written explanation of them`
    `made them available for inspection at a reasonable time and place`
    `something else, such as an oral summary or a refusal`
    `nothing yet`

GIVEN `what they did` IS A `What the provider has done`
GIVETH A `Step`
`the step taken` `what they did` MEANS
    CONSIDER `what they did`
    WHEN `gave me a copy of the data or documents` THEN `give me a copy of the data or documents`
    WHEN `gave me a meaningful written explanation of them` THEN `give me a meaningful written explanation of the data or documents`
    WHEN `made them available for inspection at a reasonable time and place` THEN `make the data or documents available for inspection at a reasonable time and place`
    WHEN `something else, such as an oral summary or a refusal` THEN `something else`
    WHEN `nothing yet` THEN `something else`

DECLARE `Access to the data answer` IS ONE OF
    `done: the insurer or fund has given you the access s 28 (3) requires`
    `still owed: the insurer or fund must give you a copy, a written explanation, or inspection (s 28 (3)); the Act sets no deadline`
    `no duty yet: s 28 (3) arises only when you ask for access to the data or documents (the text's no)`

@export Has my insurer or super fund given me the access to its data that the Act requires (Discrimination Act 1991 (ACT) s 28 (3))? If you ask for access to the actuarial or statistical data (or other documents) it relied on to discriminate in your terms, it must give you a copy, or a meaningful written explanation, or make them available for inspection at a reasonable time and place.
GIVEN `I asked for access` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have asked the insurer or fund for access to the data or documents it relied on for the terms it offered you
      `what they did` IS A `What the provider has done` @desc What the insurer or fund has done since you asked
GIVETH A `Access to the data answer`
`s 28 (3) -- has my insurer given me access to its data` `I asked for access` `what they did` MEANS
    IF NOT `I asked for access`
    THEN `no duty yet: s 28 (3) arises only when you ask for access to the data or documents (the text's no)`
    ELSE CONSIDER EVALTRACE `s 28 (3) -- give access to the data relied on` 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL 1), (EVENT `the insurer or super fund` (`the step taken` `what they did`) 1))
         WHEN FULFILLED THEN `done: the insurer or fund has given you the access s 28 (3) requires`
         OTHERWISE `still owed: the insurer or fund must give you a copy, a written explanation, or inspection (s 28 (3)); the Act sets no deadline`

-- s 32: religious bodies. (1) (a)-(c) ordination, training, religious functions: always; (d) goods, services or
-- facilities to the public and (e) employment by the body: only if on the ground of religious conviction only,
-- conforming to doctrine, necessary to avoid injury to adherents' religious susceptibilities, and under a published,
-- readily accessible policy; (f) accommodation for a relevant class; (g) any other act conforming to doctrine and
-- necessary to avoid injury. (2): (d)-(g) never for employment at, or admission or treatment of students at, an
-- educational institution. (3): not for a body whose sole or main purpose is commercial.
DECLARE `What the religious body did` IS ONE OF
    `ordaining or appointing priests, ministers or members of an order`
    `training people seeking ordination or appointment`
    `selecting people for functions in religious observance or practice`
    `providing goods, services or facilities to the public`
    `employing people`
    `providing accommodation for a class of people sharing a protected attribute`
    `some other act or practice`

@export Can a religious body rely on the religious-bodies exception for what it did to me (Discrimination Act 1991 (ACT) s 32)? Always for ordination, training for ordination and religious functions; for providing goods or services to the public, or employment, only on the ground of religious conviction alone, in conformity with its doctrines, where necessary to avoid injury to adherents' religious susceptibilities, and under a published, accessible policy. Never for employment or students at an educational institution (other than ordination, training and religious functions), and never for a body whose main purpose is commercial.
GIVEN `what it did` IS A `What the religious body did` @desc What the religious body did when it treated you unfavourably
      `mainly commercial` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the religious body's sole or main purpose is a commercial purpose
      `at an educational institution` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the matter concerns employment at an educational institution, or the admission, treatment or continued enrolment of a student at one
      `religious conviction only` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the discrimination was on the ground of religious conviction only (not, for example, sexuality or relationship status)
      `conforms to doctrine` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the discrimination conforms to the doctrines, tenets or beliefs of the body's religion
      `necessary to avoid injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the discrimination is necessary to avoid injury to the religious susceptibilities of adherents of the religion (a judgment for the Commission or the ACAT)
      `policy published and accessible` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the body has published its policy on providing goods, services or facilities (or on employment) and the policy is readily accessible to the public
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 32 -- can the religious body rely on the exception` `what it did` `mainly commercial` `at an educational institution` `religious conviction only` `conforms to doctrine` `necessary to avoid injury` `policy published and accessible` IF
        (NOT `mainly commercial`)
    AND (CONSIDER `what it did`
         WHEN `ordaining or appointing priests, ministers or members of an order` THEN TRUE
         WHEN `training people seeking ordination or appointment` THEN TRUE
         WHEN `selecting people for functions in religious observance or practice` THEN TRUE
         WHEN `providing goods, services or facilities to the public` THEN `paragraphs (d) and (e)`
         WHEN `employing people` THEN `paragraphs (d) and (e)`
         WHEN `providing accommodation for a class of people sharing a protected attribute` THEN NOT `at an educational institution`
         WHEN `some other act or practice` THEN (NOT `at an educational institution`) AND `conforms to doctrine` AND `necessary to avoid injury`)
    WHERE
        `paragraphs (d) and (e)` MEANS
                (NOT `at an educational institution`)
            AND `religious conviction only`
            AND `conforms to doctrine`
            AND `necessary to avoid injury`
            AND `policy published and accessible`

-- s 33B: in relation to a position as employee, commission agent, contract worker or business partner, if (a) it is a
-- genuine occupational qualification that the position be filled by a person with a particular protected attribute and
-- (b) the discrimination is reasonable, proportionate and justifiable; (2) not on the ground of religious conviction.
@export Can the other side say the job needed someone with a particular attribute, a genuine occupational qualification (Discrimination Act 1991 (ACT) s 33B)? Only for a position as an employee, commission agent, contract worker or partner, where having a particular protected attribute is a genuine occupational qualification and the discrimination is reasonable, proportionate and justifiable; never on the ground of religious conviction.
GIVEN `a work position` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the discrimination was about a position as an employee, commission agent, contract worker or business partner
      `the ground` IS A `Protected attribute` @desc The protected attribute you were treated unfavourably on the ground of
      `genuine occupational qualification` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is a genuine occupational qualification of the position that it be filled by a person with a particular protected attribute (for example a female carer for a woman's personal care, for privacy)
      `reasonable, proportionate and justifiable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the discrimination is reasonable, proportionate and justifiable in the circumstances (a judgment for the Commission or the ACAT)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 33B -- can they rely on a genuine occupational qualification` `a work position` `the ground` `genuine occupational qualification` `reasonable, proportionate and justifiable` IF
        `a work position`
    AND (NOT (`the ground` EQUALS `religious conviction`))
    AND `genuine occupational qualification`
    AND `reasonable, proportionate and justifiable`

-- s 33C: an employer, against "a prospective employee", in relation to a position of employment, if (a) the person is
-- or would be unable to carry out the inherent requirements of the position because of the attribute, regardless of any
-- reasonable adjustments the employer could make under s 74; and (b) reasonable, proportionate and justifiable.
-- Finding 3: the exception names only a prospective employee.
@export Can an employer refuse me a job because I could not carry out its inherent requirements (Discrimination Act 1991 (ACT) s 33C)? Only against a prospective employee who is, or would be, unable to carry out the inherent requirements of the position because of the protected attribute even with any reasonable adjustments the employer could make, and only if the discrimination is reasonable, proportionate and justifiable.
GIVEN `I am a prospective employee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were seeking the position (rather than already employed in it)
      `unable to do the inherent requirements` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, because of your protected attribute, you are or would be unable to carry out the inherent (essential) requirements of the position
      `even with reasonable adjustments` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you would still be unable to carry them out after any reasonable adjustments the employer could make (s 74)
      `reasonable, proportionate and justifiable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the discrimination is reasonable, proportionate and justifiable in the circumstances (a judgment for the Commission or the ACAT)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 33C -- can they rely on the inherent requirements of the job` `I am a prospective employee` `unable to do the inherent requirements` `even with reasonable adjustments` `reasonable, proportionate and justifiable` IF
        `I am a prospective employee`
    AND `unable to do the inherent requirements`
    AND `even with reasonable adjustments`
    AND `reasonable, proportionate and justifiable`

-- s 46: religious educational institutions. (1) refusing admission, on the ground of religious conviction, to an
-- institution conducted solely for students of another religious conviction, (3) only if it has published a policy on
-- student matters, readily accessible to prospective and current students; (2) staff matters (employing staff, engaging
-- contractors) if the institution is conducted according to a religion's doctrines and the discrimination is intended
-- to enable it to be so conducted, (4) only if it has published a policy on staff matters, readily accessible to
-- prospective and current staff and contractors.
DECLARE `School matter` IS ONE OF
    `refusing me admission as a student`
    `employing me, or engaging me as a contractor`
    `something else`

@export Can a religious school or college rely on the religious-institution exception against me (Discrimination Act 1991 (ACT) s 46)? Only on the ground of religious conviction: to refuse admission where it is conducted solely for students of another religious conviction, or in employing staff or engaging contractors where it is conducted according to a religion and the discrimination is meant to let it be so conducted; and in either case only if it has published an accessible policy on that matter.
GIVEN `the matter` IS A `School matter` @desc What the institution did: refused you admission as a student, or treated you unfavourably in employing you or engaging you as a contractor, or something else
      `ground is religious conviction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were treated unfavourably on the ground of religious conviction (yours, or your not having one)
      `solely for another religion` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For admission: whether the institution is conducted solely for students having a religious conviction other than yours
      `conducted by a religion's doctrines` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For staff: whether the institution is conducted in accordance with the doctrines, tenets, beliefs or teaching of a particular religion or creed
      `intended to enable that` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For staff: whether the discrimination is intended to enable, or better enable, the institution to be conducted in accordance with those doctrines
      `policy published and accessible` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the institution has published its policy on student admissions (for admission) or on staff matters (for staff), readily accessible to prospective and current students, or staff and contractors
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 46 -- can the religious school rely on the exception` `the matter` `ground is religious conviction` `solely for another religion` `conducted by a religion's doctrines` `intended to enable that` `policy published and accessible` IF
        `ground is religious conviction`
    AND `policy published and accessible`
    AND (CONSIDER `the matter`
         WHEN `refusing me admission as a student` THEN `solely for another religion`
         WHEN `employing me, or engaging me as a contractor` THEN `conducted by a religion's doctrines` AND `intended to enable that`
         WHEN `something else` THEN FALSE)

-- ss 51 (2), 52, 53, 54: disability and unjustifiable hardship. s 52 (1): inaccessible premises, if any alteration would
-- impose unjustifiable hardship, (4) not for a building whose construction began on or after a day the Minister
-- declares (s 52 (2)); s 53: goods, services or facilities that would have to be provided in a special way; s 54:
-- accommodation needing special services or facilities; s 51 (2): admission to an educational institution needing
-- services or facilities other students do not need. s 5B: unjustifiable hardship weighs all relevant circumstances,
-- including benefit and detriment to each person, the attributes, and the cost and financial circumstances.
DECLARE `Disability setting` IS ONE OF
    `access to premises (s 52)`
    `goods, services or facilities (s 53)`
    `accommodation (s 54)`
    `admission as a student (s 51 (2))`

@export Can the other side refuse to accommodate my disability because it would cause them unjustifiable hardship (Discrimination Act 1991 (ACT) ss 51 (2), 52, 53, 54, 5B)? Only where, because of your disability, the premises would need altering (and the building is not one the Minister has declared outside s 52), or the goods, services or facilities would have to be provided in a special way, or accommodation or study would need special services or facilities, and doing that would impose unjustifiable hardship on them.
GIVEN `the setting` IS A `Disability setting` @desc Where the refusal happened: access to premises, goods, services or facilities, accommodation, or admission as a student
      `something special is needed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, because of your disability, the premises are inaccessible to you as designed or built (premises), or the goods, services, facilities, accommodation or education would have to be provided with something special that people without the disability do not need
      `unjustifiable hardship` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether providing it would impose unjustifiable hardship on them, weighing the benefit and detriment to each of you, your attributes, and their cost and financial circumstances (s 5B; a judgment for the Commission or the ACAT)
      `building declared by the Minister` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For premises only: whether the building's construction (building approval) began on or after a day the Minister has declared under s 52 (2); FALSE if you do not know of any declaration
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `ss 51-54 -- can they rely on unjustifiable hardship` `the setting` `something special is needed` `unjustifiable hardship` `building declared by the Minister` IF
        `something special is needed`
    AND `unjustifiable hardship`
    AND (NOT (`the setting` EQUALS `access to premises (s 52)` AND `building declared by the Minister`))

-- s 57B: if an award (a modern award under the Fair Work Act 2009) provides reduced wages for employees under 21,
-- s 10 (1) or (2) (a) or (b) does not make age discrimination unlawful (a) against a person 21 or older in offering
-- employment to a person under 21; (b) against a person under 21 in paying reduced wages in accordance with the award.
DECLARE `Youth wages matter` IS ONE OF
    `the job was offered to someone under 21 instead of me`
    `I am paid reduced wages under the award`
    `something else`

@export Can my employer pay me youth wages, or prefer a younger applicant because of youth wages (Discrimination Act 1991 (ACT) s 57B)? Only where a modern award provides reduced wages for employees under 21: an employer may offer a job to a person under 21 rather than to you if you are 21 or older, and may pay you reduced wages under the award while you are under 21.
GIVEN `award has youth wages` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a modern award under the Fair Work Act 2009 provides for reduced wages for employees under 21 years old in your job
      `my age` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in whole years
      `the matter` IS A `Youth wages matter` @desc What happened: the job was offered to someone under 21 instead of you, or you are being paid reduced wages under the award, or something else
      `paid in accordance with the award` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For reduced wages: whether the reduced wages are paid in accordance with the award
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 57B -- can my employer rely on youth wages` `award has youth wages` `my age` `the matter` `paid in accordance with the award` IF
        `award has youth wages`
    AND (CONSIDER `the matter`
         WHEN `the job was offered to someone under 21 instead of me` THEN `my age` AT LEAST 21
         WHEN `I am paid reduced wages under the award` THEN `my age` LESS THAN 21 AND `paid in accordance with the award`
         WHEN `something else` THEN FALSE)

-- ss 41, 57, 57M: formally organised competitive sport. s 41: excluding people of one sex, if strength, stamina or
-- physique is relevant and the discrimination is reasonable, proportionate and justifiable; s 41 (2): not for coaching,
-- umpiring or refereeing, administration, prescribed activities, or a child under 12. s 57: disability, if reasonable,
-- proportionate and justifiable and the person is not reasonably capable, or competitors are selected reasonably on
-- skills; or the activity is for people with a particular disability the person does not have. s 57M: limiting
-- participation to an age group. ss 57 (2), 57M (2): not for coaching, umpiring, administration or prescribed activities.
DECLARE `Sport ground` IS ONE OF
    `my sex`
    `my disability`
    `my age`
    `another ground`

DECLARE `My part in the sport` IS ONE OF
    `competing`
    `coaching`
    `umpiring or refereeing`
    `administering the sport`
    `competing in an activity the regulation prescribes`

@export Can I lawfully be excluded from a formally organised competitive sport because of my sex, disability or age (Discrimination Act 1991 (ACT) ss 41, 57, 57M)? Only as a competitor (never from coaching, umpiring, refereeing or administration): by sex where strength, stamina or physique matters, the exclusion is reasonable, proportionate and justifiable and you are 12 or older; by disability where it is reasonable, proportionate and justifiable and you cannot reasonably perform or selection is reasonably by skill, or the event is for people with a disability you do not have; by age where the event is limited to an age group.
GIVEN `the ground` IS A `Sport ground` @desc The ground on which you were excluded: your sex, your disability, your age, or another ground
      `formally organised competitive` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the activity is a formally organised competitive sporting activity (for example a league or club competition, not backyard cricket)
      `my part` IS A `My part in the sport` @desc What you were excluded from: competing, coaching, umpiring or refereeing, administering, or competing in an activity the Discrimination Regulation prescribes
      `my age` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in whole years
      `strength, stamina or physique relevant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For sex: whether the strength, stamina or physique of competitors is relevant
      `reasonable, proportionate and justifiable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For sex or disability: whether the exclusion is reasonable, proportionate and justifiable in the circumstances (a judgment for the Commission or the ACAT)
      `not reasonably capable or selected on skill` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For disability: whether you are not reasonably capable of the actions reasonably required, or the participants are selected in a reasonable way on their relevant skills and abilities relative to each other
      `event for a disability I do not have` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For disability: whether the activity is conducted for, or mainly for, people with a particular kind of disability that you do not have
      `limited to an age group` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For age: whether participation is limited to people of a particular age group (for example under-15s or veterans)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `ss 41, 57, 57M -- can I be excluded from the sport` `the ground` `formally organised competitive` `my part` `my age` `strength, stamina or physique relevant` `reasonable, proportionate and justifiable` `not reasonably capable or selected on skill` `event for a disability I do not have` `limited to an age group` IF
        `formally organised competitive`
    AND `my part` EQUALS `competing`
    AND (CONSIDER `the ground`
         WHEN `my sex` THEN `strength, stamina or physique relevant` AND `reasonable, proportionate and justifiable` AND `my age` AT LEAST 12
         WHEN `my disability` THEN (`reasonable, proportionate and justifiable` AND `not reasonably capable or selected on skill`) OR `event for a disability I do not have`
         WHEN `my age` THEN `limited to an age group`
         WHEN `another ground` THEN FALSE)

§§ `Unlawful discrimination, put together (Parts 2-4, ss 30, 70-72, 109)`

DECLARE `Unlawful discrimination answer` IS ONE OF
    `unlawful discrimination: you may complain to the Human Rights Commission (this Act itself gives no right to sue, s 72, and it is not an offence, s 71)`
    `not unlawful: what was done is not discrimination under s 8 (the text's no)`
    `not unlawful: s 23C (2) takes this act of a public authority outside Part 3 (the text's no)`
    `not shown: Part 3 does not reach this area or this act (NOTHING-kind)`
    `not unlawful: it was done necessarily to comply with a territory law, a determination or direction under one, or a court or ACAT order (s 30) (the text's no)`
    `not unlawful: the other side has established a Part 4 exception (the text's no)`
    `not unlawful: the other side holds an exemption from the Human Rights Commission under s 109 (the text's no)`

-- s 8 discrimination, in an area and by an act Part 3 lists, and none of: s 30 (statutory authority), a Part 4 exception
-- established by the other side (s 70: the onus is on them), a s 109 exemption. s 30 is checked before the other
-- exceptions because it applies to the whole Act.
@export Is what was done to me unlawful discrimination under the Discrimination Act 1991 (ACT)? It is if it was direct or indirect discrimination on a protected attribute (s 8), in an area and by an act Part 3 covers (work, education, premises, goods and services, accommodation, clubs, sport, competitions, ACT public authorities), unless it was done necessarily to comply with a law or order (s 30), the other side establishes a Part 4 exception (s 70), or holds an exemption (s 109). An unlawful act can be the subject of a complaint to the ACT Human Rights Commission.
GIVEN `unfavourable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the other person treated you, or proposed to treat you, unfavourably (including by failing or refusing to do something)
      `because of it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they did so because you have one or more protected attributes (it is enough that the attribute was one of the reasons)
      `condition imposed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they imposed, or proposed to impose, a condition or requirement on you (a rule, policy or practice)
      `condition disadvantages me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that condition has, or is likely to have, the effect of disadvantaging you because you have a protected attribute
      `condition reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the condition is reasonable in the circumstances (a judgment for the Commission or the ACAT, s 8 (5))
      `the area` IS AN `Area of life` @desc Who discriminated against you, and in what capacity (your employer, a school, a shop, a landlord, a club, a public authority and so on); "none of these areas" for a private or social setting
      `a listed act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether what they did is one of the acts that Part 3 section lists (refusing, the terms or conditions, denying a benefit, dismissing, expelling, evicting, or any other detriment)
      `s 23C (2) excludes it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a public authority only: whether the act was in a Legislative Assembly proceeding, a court's or tribunal's non-administrative act, or an education matter under s 18; otherwise FALSE
      `done to comply with a law or order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether what was done was done necessarily to comply with a requirement of a territory law, a determination or direction under one, or an order of a court or the ACAT
      `they establish an exception` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the other side establishes that a Part 4 exception applies (they bear the onus, s 70; see the questions on each exception)
      `they hold an exemption` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the other side holds an exemption from the Human Rights Commission under s 109 that covers what was done (see the exemption question)
GIVETH A `Unlawful discrimination answer`
`is what was done to me unlawful discrimination` `unfavourable` `because of it` `condition imposed` `condition disadvantages me` `condition reasonable` `the area` `a listed act` `s 23C (2) excludes it` `done to comply with a law or order` `they establish an exception` `they hold an exemption` MEANS
    IF `no discrimination`
    THEN `not unlawful: what was done is not discrimination under s 8 (the text's no)`
    ELSE CONSIDER `the Part 3 coverage` `the area` `a listed act` `s 23C (2) excludes it`
         WHEN `not covered: s 23C (2) takes this act of a public authority outside s 23C (the text's no)` THEN `not unlawful: s 23C (2) takes this act of a public authority outside Part 3 (the text's no)`
         WHEN `not covered: Part 3 does not reach this area or this act (NOTHING-kind)` THEN `not shown: Part 3 does not reach this area or this act (NOTHING-kind)`
         WHEN `covered: Part 3 makes discrimination in this area, by this act, unlawful (unless an exception or exemption applies)` THEN
            IF `done to comply with a law or order`
            THEN `not unlawful: it was done necessarily to comply with a territory law, a determination or direction under one, or a court or ACAT order (s 30) (the text's no)`
            ELSE IF `they establish an exception`
                 THEN `not unlawful: the other side has established a Part 4 exception (the text's no)`
                 ELSE IF `they hold an exemption`
                      THEN `not unlawful: the other side holds an exemption from the Human Rights Commission under s 109 (the text's no)`
                      ELSE `unlawful discrimination: you may complain to the Human Rights Commission (this Act itself gives no right to sue, s 72, and it is not an offence, s 71)`
    WHERE
        `the kind found` MEANS `the kind of discrimination` `unfavourable` `because of it` `condition imposed` `condition disadvantages me` `condition reasonable`
        `no discrimination` MEANS
                `the kind found` EQUALS `no discrimination: the condition is reasonable in the circumstances (s 8 (4)) (the text's no)`
            OR  `the kind found` EQUALS `no discrimination under s 8 (the text's no)`

§§ `Part 10 -- Exemptions (s 109)`

-- s 109 (1), (2): the Human Rights Commission may exempt an applicant from a specified provision of Part 3, 5 or 7;
-- (4) (b) subject to its conditions; (4) (c) "has effect according to its terms for the period not longer than 3 years
-- specified in the notice". Fork F8: a notice stating a longer period is read as having effect for 3 years only; the
-- 3 years run from the day the exemption starts, the last day being the day before the third anniversary.
@export Does the other side's exemption from the Human Rights Commission cover what they did to me (Discrimination Act 1991 (ACT) s 109)? Only if the exemption is from the provision that would make what they did unlawful, what they did was done while the exemption had effect (for the period stated in the notice, never longer than 3 years), and they complied with its conditions.
GIVEN `exempt from that provision` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the exemption (or further exemption) is from the provision of Part 3, 5 or 7 that would make what they did unlawful
      `exemption starts on` IS A DATE @desc The day the exemption started to have effect, as stated in the notice
      `exemption ends on` IS A DATE @desc The last day of the period stated in the notice
      `done on` IS A DATE @desc The day they did what you complain of
      `conditions met` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they complied with the conditions (if any) stated in the exemption notice
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 109 -- does their exemption cover what they did` `exempt from that provision` `exemption starts on` `exemption ends on` `done on` `conditions met` IF
        `exempt from that provision`
    AND `conditions met`
    AND `done on` `is on or after` `exemption starts on`
    AND (NOT (`exemption ends on` `falls before` `done on`))
    AND `done on` `falls before` (`exemption starts on` `plus years` 3)

§§ `Part 5 -- Sexual harassment (ss 58-67)`

DECLARE `What they did` IS ONE OF
    `an unwelcome sexual advance`
    `an unwelcome request for sexual favours`
    `other unwelcome conduct of a sexual nature, including a sexual statement to me or in my presence, spoken or written`
    `none of these acts`

-- s 58: "a person subjects someone else to sexual harassment if the person makes an unwelcome sexual advance, or an
-- unwelcome request for sexual favours, ... or engages in other unwelcome conduct of a sexual nature in circumstances in
-- which the other person reasonably feels offended, humiliated or intimidated." Fork F4: the "circumstances" clause read
-- as qualifying all three limbs. A definition: outside it, the text's no.
@export Was what happened to me sexual harassment (Discrimination Act 1991 (ACT) s 58)? It is if someone made an unwelcome sexual advance or request for sexual favours, or engaged in other unwelcome conduct of a sexual nature (including a sexual statement to you or in your presence), in circumstances in which you reasonably felt offended, humiliated or intimidated.
GIVEN `what they did` IS A `What they did` @desc What the other person did
      `I reasonably felt offended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it happened in circumstances in which you reasonably felt offended, humiliated or intimidated (a judgment for the Commission or the ACAT)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 58 -- was it sexual harassment` `what they did` `I reasonably felt offended` IF
        (NOT (`what they did` EQUALS `none of these acts`))
    AND `I reasonably felt offended`

-- ss 59-67: who may not sexually harass whom.
DECLARE `Who harassed me` IS ONE OF
    `s 59 (1): my employer, and I am their employee or sought employment with them`
    `s 59 (2): a fellow employee, or an employee of the employer I sought to work for`
    `s 59 (3): a principal, and I am or sought to be their commission agent or contract worker`
    `s 59 (4): a fellow commission agent or contract worker`
    `s 59 (5): a partner in a partnership I am in or sought to join`
    `s 59 (6): another workplace participant, at a place that is a workplace (or potential workplace) of us both`
    `s 60 (1): a member of staff of the educational institution where I study or sought admission`
    `s 60 (2): a student at the educational institution where I study or am on the staff`
    `s 61: a person providing or offering me access to premises open to the public`
    `s 62: a person providing or offering me goods, services or facilities`
    `s 63: a person providing or offering me accommodation`
    `s 64: a member of the committee of management of a club I belong to or sought to join`
    `s 65: a person organising, administering or taking part in a sporting activity I take part in`
    `s 66: a person organising, administering or taking part in a competition I take part in`
    `s 67: a person administering a territory law or an ACT government program or policy`
    `someone else, in another setting`

DECLARE `Sexual harassment answer` IS ONE OF
    `unlawful sexual harassment: you may complain to the Human Rights Commission`
    `not unlawful: what happened is not sexual harassment under s 58 (the text's no)`
    `not shown: Part 5 does not reach sexual harassment by this person in this setting (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Was I unlawfully sexually harassed (Discrimination Act 1991 (ACT) ss 58-67)? Sexual harassment is unlawful at work (by an employer, fellow employee, principal, fellow contract worker or agent, partner, or any workplace participant at a shared workplace), in education (by staff or students), in providing access to premises, goods, services, facilities or accommodation, in clubs, sport and competitions, and in administering ACT laws and programs.
GIVEN `what they did` IS A `What they did` @desc What the other person did
      `I reasonably felt offended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it happened in circumstances in which you reasonably felt offended, humiliated or intimidated (a judgment for the Commission or the ACAT)
      `who` IS A `Who harassed me` @desc Who the person was in relation to you, and the setting
GIVETH A `Sexual harassment answer`
`ss 59-67 -- was I unlawfully sexually harassed` `what they did` `I reasonably felt offended` `who` MEANS
    IF NOT `s 58 -- was it sexual harassment` `what they did` `I reasonably felt offended`
    THEN `not unlawful: what happened is not sexual harassment under s 58 (the text's no)`
    ELSE IF `who` EQUALS `someone else, in another setting`
         THEN `not shown: Part 5 does not reach sexual harassment by this person in this setting (NOTHING-kind)`
         ELSE `unlawful sexual harassment: you may complain to the Human Rights Commission`

§§ `Part 7 -- Vilification, victimisation and advertising (ss 67A-69)`

DECLARE `Vilification ground` IS ONE OF
    `on the ground of disability`
    `on the ground of gender identity`
    `on the ground of HIV/AIDS status`
    `on the ground of race`
    `on the ground of religious conviction`
    `on the ground of sex`
    `on the ground of sex characteristics`
    `on the ground of sexuality`
    `on the ground of association with a person or group identified by one of these`
    `on another ground`

DECLARE `Vilification defence` IS ONE OF
    `no defence of this kind`
    `it was a fair report of such an act`
    `it was communicating a publication protected by absolute privilege in defamation`
    `it was done reasonably and honestly for academic, artistic, scientific, research or other public-interest purposes`

DECLARE `Vilification answer` IS ONE OF
    `unlawful vilification: you may complain to the Human Rights Commission (serious vilification is also an offence, Criminal Code s 750)`
    `not unlawful: s 67A (2) says this is not unlawful (the text's no)`
    `not shown: s 67A (1) does not reach it -- another ground, in private, or not inciting hatred, revulsion, serious contempt or severe ridicule (NOTHING-kind)`

-- s 67A (1): inciting hatred toward, revulsion of, serious contempt for, or severe ridicule of a person or group on a
-- listed ground, other than in private. (2) "However, it is not unlawful to" -- a flat negative: the text's no.
@export Was I (or a group I belong to) unlawfully vilified (Discrimination Act 1991 (ACT) s 67A)? It is unlawful, other than in private, to incite hatred toward, revulsion of, serious contempt for, or severe ridicule of a person or group on the ground of disability, gender identity, HIV/AIDS status, race, religious conviction, sex, sex characteristics or sexuality, or association with such people, unless it was a fair report, a privileged publication, or done reasonably and honestly for academic, artistic, scientific, research or public-interest purposes.
GIVEN `the ground` IS A `Vilification ground` @desc The ground on which you or your group were targeted
      `not in private` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it happened other than in private (for example a public social media post, a public event, a broadcast interview, or signs, clothes or gestures the public could see)
      `incites` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the act incited hatred toward, revulsion of, serious contempt for, or severe ridicule of you or your group (a judgment for the Commission or the ACAT)
      `defence` IS A `Vilification defence` @desc Whether the act was a fair report, the communication of a publication protected by absolute privilege, or done reasonably and honestly for academic, artistic, scientific, research or other public-interest purposes; "no defence of this kind" otherwise
GIVETH A `Vilification answer`
`s 67A -- was I unlawfully vilified` `the ground` `not in private` `incites` `defence` MEANS
    IF `the ground` EQUALS `on another ground` OR (NOT `not in private`) OR (NOT `incites`)
    THEN `not shown: s 67A (1) does not reach it -- another ground, in private, or not inciting hatred, revulsion, serious contempt or severe ridicule (NOTHING-kind)`
    ELSE IF `defence` EQUALS `no defence of this kind`
         THEN `unlawful vilification: you may complain to the Human Rights Commission (serious vilification is also an offence, Criminal Code s 750)`
         ELSE `not unlawful: s 67A (2) says this is not unlawful (the text's no)`

-- s 68 (2): "discrimination action".
DECLARE `Discrimination action` IS ONE OF
    `beginning a proceeding in the ACAT or a court about this Act`
    `making a discrimination complaint to the Human Rights Commission`
    `taking part in or helping a criminal investigation of serious vilification`
    `giving information or documents to a person acting under the Human Rights Commission Act about a complaint`
    `giving information, documents or answers when required under the Human Rights Commission Act about a complaint`
    `giving evidence or documents to the ACAT or a court about this Act`
    `reasonably asserting rights under this Act`
    `claiming that someone did an unlawful act or committed serious vilification`
    `doing anything else in accordance with this Act`
    `no discrimination action`

DECLARE `Victimisation answer` IS ONE OF
    `unlawful victimisation: you may complain to the Human Rights Commission`
    `not victimisation: a claim that is false and not made honestly is not discrimination action (s 68 (2) (h)) (the text's no)`
    `not shown: s 68 does not reach it -- no detriment or threat, no discrimination action, or not because of it (NOTHING-kind)`

-- s 68 (1): subjecting, or threatening to subject, a person to any detriment because the person, or someone associated
-- with them, has taken or proposes to take discrimination action, or because the first person believes so. Fork F5: the
-- "other than a claim that is false and not made honestly" carve-out in (h), once met, is the text's no.
@export Was I victimised for taking action under the Discrimination Act 1991 (ACT) (s 68)? It is unlawful to subject you, or threaten to subject you, to any detriment because you (or someone associated with you) made a discrimination complaint, began a proceeding, gave information or evidence, reasonably asserted rights, claimed someone acted unlawfully (unless the claim was false and not made honestly), or did anything else under the Act, or proposed to, or because they believe you did or proposed to.
GIVEN `detriment or threat` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the other person subjected you to a detriment (for example dismissal, demotion, eviction, exclusion) or threatened to
      `the action` IS A `Discrimination action` @desc The action you (or someone associated with you) took or proposed to take, or that the other person believed was taken or proposed
      `because of it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they did or threatened it because of that action (it is enough that it was one of the reasons, s 4A (2))
      `claim false and dishonest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only where the action was a claim that someone acted unlawfully: whether that claim was both false and not made honestly; otherwise FALSE
GIVETH A `Victimisation answer`
`s 68 -- was I victimised` `detriment or threat` `the action` `because of it` `claim false and dishonest` MEANS
    IF `the action` EQUALS `claiming that someone did an unlawful act or committed serious vilification` AND `claim false and dishonest`
    THEN `not victimisation: a claim that is false and not made honestly is not discrimination action (s 68 (2) (h)) (the text's no)`
    ELSE IF `detriment or threat` AND `because of it` AND (NOT (`the action` EQUALS `no discrimination action`))
         THEN `unlawful victimisation: you may complain to the Human Rights Commission`
         ELSE `not shown: s 68 does not reach it -- no detriment or threat, no discrimination action, or not because of it (NOTHING-kind)`

DECLARE `Advertising answer` IS ONE OF
    `unlawful advertising (s 69): you may complain to the Human Rights Commission`
    `not shown: s 69 does not reach an advertisement that does not indicate such an intention (NOTHING-kind)`

-- s 69: advertising any matter that indicates, or could reasonably be understood as indicating, an intention to do an
-- act unlawful under Part 3, 5 or 7.
@export Is an advertisement I saw unlawful (Discrimination Act 1991 (ACT) s 69)? It is unlawful to advertise anything that indicates, or could reasonably be understood as indicating, an intention to do an act that is unlawful discrimination, sexual harassment, vilification or victimisation (for example a job ad saying "no one over 50").
GIVEN `indicates an intention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the advertisement indicates an intention to do something that would be unlawful discrimination, sexual harassment, vilification or victimisation under the Act
      `could reasonably be understood so` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the advertisement could reasonably be understood as indicating such an intention, even if not stated outright
GIVETH A `Advertising answer`
`s 69 -- is the advertisement unlawful` `indicates an intention` `could reasonably be understood so` MEANS
    IF `indicates an intention` OR `could reasonably be understood so`
    THEN `unlawful advertising (s 69): you may complain to the Human Rights Commission`
    ELSE `not shown: s 69 does not reach an advertisement that does not indicate such an intention (NOTHING-kind)`

§§ `Part 8 and s 121A -- Who is answerable (ss 73, 121A)`

DECLARE `Answerable for a representative answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the employer or principal is taken to have done it too (s 121A (2))`
    `no: they took all reasonable steps to prevent it (s 121A (3)) (the text's no)`
    `no: the conduct was not by their employee or agent within the scope of their authority (s 121A (2)) (the text's no)`

-- s 121A (2): conduct engaged in on behalf of a person by a representative (an employee or agent) is taken to have been
-- engaged in by the person if within the representative's actual or apparent authority; (3) not if the person
-- establishes that it took all reasonable steps to prevent it.
@export Is the employer (or principal) of the person who discriminated against, harassed or victimised me also answerable for it (Discrimination Act 1991 (ACT) s 121A)? Yes if the person was their employee or agent acting on their behalf within the scope of their actual or apparent authority, unless the employer or principal establishes that it took all reasonable steps to prevent the conduct.
GIVEN `an employee or agent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who did it was an employee or agent of the employer or principal you want to hold answerable, acting on its behalf
      `within their authority` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the conduct was within the scope of that employee's or agent's actual or apparent authority
      `all reasonable steps taken` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the employer or principal establishes that it took all reasonable steps to prevent the conduct (for example policies, training and complaint handling; a judgment for the Commission or the ACAT)
GIVETH A `Answerable for a representative answer`
`s 121A -- is the employer answerable too` `an employee or agent` `within their authority` `all reasonable steps taken` MEANS
    IF NOT (`an employee or agent` AND `within their authority`)
    THEN `no: the conduct was not by their employee or agent within the scope of their authority (s 121A (2)) (the text's no)`
    ELSE IF `all reasonable steps taken`
         THEN `no: they took all reasonable steps to prevent it (s 121A (3)) (the text's no)`
         ELSE `yes: the employer or principal is taken to have done it too (s 121A (2))`

DECLARE `Accessory answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: they are taken to have done the unlawful act too (s 73)`
    `not shown: s 73 does not reach someone who did not aid, abet, counsel or procure it (NOTHING-kind)`

-- s 73: a person who aids, abets, counsels or procures someone else to do an unlawful act is taken also to have done it.
@export Is someone who helped or encouraged the unlawful act also taken to have done it (Discrimination Act 1991 (ACT) s 73)? Yes: a person who aids, abets, counsels or procures someone else to do an unlawful act is taken, for the Act, also to have done it.
GIVEN `aided or procured it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person aided, abetted, counselled or procured (helped, encouraged, advised or brought about) the unlawful act done to you
GIVETH A `Accessory answer`
`s 73 -- is the helper taken to have done it` `aided or procured it` MEANS
    IF `aided or procured it`
    THEN `yes: they are taken to have done the unlawful act too (s 73)`
    ELSE `not shown: s 73 does not reach someone who did not aid, abet, counsel or procure it (NOTHING-kind)`

§§ `Part 9 -- Positive duties (ss 74-76)`

DECLARE `Reasonable adjustments answer` IS ONE OF
    `unlawful: failing to make reasonable adjustments for you is an unlawful act (s 74 (3))`
    `no breach: they made reasonable adjustments for your needs`
    `no breach: the adjustment would cause them unjustifiable hardship, so it is not a reasonable adjustment (s 74 (2)) (the text's no)`
    `no duty: discrimination on that ground is not unlawful here -- outside Part 3, or an exception or exemption applies (ss 74 (1), 76) (the text's no)`
    `no duty: you have no particular need arising from a protected attribute (s 74 (1)) (the text's no)`

-- s 74 (1): a person must make reasonable adjustments to accommodate another person's particular needs arising from a
-- protected attribute "if discrimination on the ground of the attribute is unlawful under this Act"; (2) not reasonable
-- if it would cause unjustifiable hardship; (3) failure is an unlawful act. s 76: the duty does not apply where a Part 4
-- exception makes the discrimination lawful or a Part 10 exemption applies. Fork F6: "unlawful under this Act" read as:
-- the area and the act are covered by Part 3 (or Part 5 or 7) and no exception or exemption applies.
@export Did the other side break the law by not making reasonable adjustments for my needs (Discrimination Act 1991 (ACT) ss 74, 76)? A person must make reasonable adjustments for your particular needs arising from a protected attribute wherever discrimination on that ground would be unlawful (in work, education, services, accommodation and the other Part 3 areas) and no exception or exemption applies; an adjustment that would cause them unjustifiable hardship is not reasonable. Failing to make one is an unlawful act.
GIVEN `I have particular needs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have particular needs arising from a protected attribute (for example a ramp, flexible hours for caring responsibilities, a modified uniform)
      `the area` IS AN `Area of life` @desc Who the other side is in relation to you, and in what capacity (your employer, a school, a shop, a landlord and so on)
      `an exception or exemption applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a Part 4 exception makes discrimination on that ground lawful in your case, or the other side holds a Part 10 exemption (see those questions)
      `they made reasonable adjustments` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they made reasonable adjustments to accommodate your needs
      `unjustifiable hardship` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the adjustment you needed would cause them unjustifiable hardship, weighing the benefit and detriment to each of you, your attributes, and their cost and financial circumstances (s 5B; a judgment for the Commission or the ACAT)
GIVETH A `Reasonable adjustments answer`
`s 74 -- did they fail to make reasonable adjustments for me` `I have particular needs` `the area` `an exception or exemption applies` `they made reasonable adjustments` `unjustifiable hardship` MEANS
    IF NOT `I have particular needs`
    THEN `no duty: you have no particular need arising from a protected attribute (s 74 (1)) (the text's no)`
    ELSE IF (NOT (`the area is in Part 3` `the area`)) OR `an exception or exemption applies`
         THEN `no duty: discrimination on that ground is not unlawful here -- outside Part 3, or an exception or exemption applies (ss 74 (1), 76) (the text's no)`
         ELSE IF `they made reasonable adjustments`
              THEN `no breach: they made reasonable adjustments for your needs`
              ELSE IF `unjustifiable hardship`
                   THEN `no breach: the adjustment would cause them unjustifiable hardship, so it is not a reasonable adjustment (s 74 (2)) (the text's no)`
                   ELSE `unlawful: failing to make reasonable adjustments for you is an unlawful act (s 74 (3))`

-- s 75 (4): the positive duty does not apply (a) to an administrative unit, territory authority or territory
-- instrumentality until 12 months after the commencement day; (b) to an individual with organisational management
-- responsibility for one until then; (c) to any other person until 3 years after the commencement day. s 75 (6):
-- commencement day is the day the Discrimination Amendment Act 2023 s 3 commenced: 11 April 2024 (endnote 3: A2023-7,
-- "remainder commenced 11 April 2024"). Fork F7: "until 12 months after the commencement day" read as the 12 months
-- running from the day after commencement (Legislation Act 2001 s 151, not deposited): the duty first applies on
-- 12 April 2025, and for others on 12 April 2027; the other reading is 11 April 2025 and 11 April 2027.
DECLARE `Who owes the positive duty` IS ONE OF
    `an ACT government directorate, territory authority or territory instrumentality`
    `an individual who controls or directs one of those`
    `any other organisation or business, or an individual who controls or directs one`

`the commencement day` MEANS `the calendar date` 11 4 2024

GIVEN `who` IS A `Who owes the positive duty`
GIVETH A DATE
`the first day the positive duty applies` `who` MEANS
    CONSIDER `who`
    WHEN `an ACT government directorate, territory authority or territory instrumentality` THEN `the calendar date` 12 4 2025
    WHEN `an individual who controls or directs one of those` THEN `the calendar date` 12 4 2025
    WHEN `any other organisation or business, or an individual who controls or directs one` THEN `the calendar date` 12 4 2027

@export From what day must this organisation or business take reasonable and proportionate steps to eliminate discrimination, sexual harassment and vilification (Discrimination Act 1991 (ACT) s 75 (4))? The positive duty in s 75 began for ACT government bodies (and those who control them) 12 months after 11 April 2024, and for every other organisation, business and manager 3 years after that day.
GIVEN `who` IS A `Who owes the positive duty` @desc What kind of organisation or person the duty-holder is: an ACT government body, an individual who controls one, or any other organisation, business or its controller (a sole trader, a company, a club, a school, a church)
GIVETH A DATE
`s 75 (4) -- from when does the positive duty apply` `who` MEANS
    `the first day the positive duty applies` `who`

DECLARE `Positive duty answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: they must take reasonable and proportionate steps to eliminate the discrimination, sexual harassment and vilification (s 75 (2))`
    `not yet: s 75 (4) puts off the duty for them until a later day (the text's no)`
    `no: s 75 applies only to an organisation, business or manager required by the Act not to discriminate, harass or vilify in the circumstances (s 75 (1)) (the text's no)`
    `no: a Part 4 exception or a Part 10 exemption applies (s 76) (the text's no)`

-- s 75 (1): the section applies to an organisation or business (and an individual with organisational management
-- responsibility) required under the Act not to engage in discrimination, sexual harassment or vilification in
-- particular circumstances; s 76: not where an exception or exemption applies.
@export Did this organisation or business owe a duty, on the day in question, to take reasonable and proportionate steps to eliminate discrimination, sexual harassment and vilification (Discrimination Act 1991 (ACT) ss 75, 76)? It does if the Act requires it not to discriminate, harass or vilify in the circumstances, no exception or exemption applies, and the duty had begun for it (12 April 2025 for ACT government bodies, 12 April 2027 for everyone else). The Act does not make a failure under s 75 an unlawful act.
GIVEN `who` IS A `Who owes the positive duty` @desc What kind of organisation or person the duty-holder is: an ACT government body, an individual who controls one, or any other organisation, business or its controller
      `bound by the Act here` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Act requires the organisation or business not to discriminate, sexually harass or vilify in these circumstances (for example as an employer, school, shop, landlord, club or sporting body)
      `an exception or exemption applies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a Part 4 exception makes the discrimination lawful, or the organisation holds a Part 10 exemption
      `on` IS A DATE @desc The day you are asking about
GIVETH A `Positive duty answer`
`s 75 -- did they owe the positive duty on that day` `who` `bound by the Act here` `an exception or exemption applies` `on` MEANS
    IF NOT `bound by the Act here`
    THEN `no: s 75 applies only to an organisation, business or manager required by the Act not to discriminate, harass or vilify in the circumstances (s 75 (1)) (the text's no)`
    ELSE IF `an exception or exemption applies`
         THEN `no: a Part 4 exception or a Part 10 exemption applies (s 76) (the text's no)`
         ELSE IF `on` `falls before` (`the first day the positive duty applies` `who`)
              THEN `not yet: s 75 (4) puts off the duty for them until a later day (the text's no)`
              ELSE `yes: they must take reasonable and proportionate steps to eliminate the discrimination, sexual harassment and vilification (s 75 (2))`
